      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What is the difference between fractional and ablative treatments?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the difference between fractional and blative treatments?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Fractional and ablative CO2 laser treatments differ primarily in how they affect the skin and their recovery. Ablative CO2 lasers completely remove the entire top layer of skin in the treated area, leading to intense treatments, longer downtimes (minimum two weeks), and often requiring general anesthesia. In contrast, fractional CO2 lasers deliver light in a pixelated pattern, treating only a fraction of the skin while sparing surrounding tissue. This makes them less aggressive, with shorter downtimes (a few days), and typically only local anesthesia. While ablative often gives results in one session, fractional usually requires a series of treatments.",
          "citations": {
