    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Okay. What is the difference between fractional and ablative treatments?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the difference between fractional and blative treatments?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The main difference between fully ablative and fractional CO2 laser treatments lies in how they target the skin.\n\n**Fully Ablative CO2 lasers** remove the entire top layer of skin in the treated area, deeply heating the underlying dermis. This is more intense, typically requires general anesthesia, and has a longer downtime (minimum two weeks). Results are often dramatic in a single session but carry higher risks like pigmentation issues and are best for fair skin.\n\n**Fractional CO2 lasers** treat only a fraction of the skin in a pixelated pattern, creating microscopic columns of treated tissue surrounded by untouched healthy skin. This is less aggressive, has minimal downtime (a few days), and usually requires a series of treatments for optimal results. They offer reduced pain and risks.",
          "citations": {
