            "clueweb22-en0033-91-06135:11": -4.139296531677246,
            "clueweb22-en0033-91-06135:12": -4.193066596984863
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Perfect summary! Since I prefer minimal downtime, it sounds like microdermabrasion or light microneedling might be safer for me. Is Mephzotheraphy better than micro-needling for pores and pigment?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is Mephzotheraphy better than micro-needling?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Quick note: did you mean \"mesotherapy\"? If so \u2014 short answer: microneedling is generally the better first choice for enlarged pores and skin texture, while mesotherapy (injecting cocktails like vitamin C, tranexamic acid, or peptides) can help with pigment depending on the injected agents but has less robust evidence.  Why microneedling may suit you: - Stimulates collagen remodeling and tightens pores.   - Lower downtime (usually 1\u20133 days) and predictable, gradual improvement with multiple sessions.   - Can be combined safely (by a pro) with topical lighteners after healing.  Mesotherapy considerations: - May reduce pigmentation if the clinician uses proven actives, but results vary.   - Involves injections \u2192 possible bruising, inflammation, and similar PIH risk if your skin is reactive.  Practical steps: - Ask for a test spot, choose a clinician experienced with darker/PIH\u2011prone skin, and insist on nut\u2011free products.   - Start with microneedling or mild in\u2011office peels before trying injectables.",
          "citations": {
